I think that’s a good description of what rich conversation often feels like, with one important qualification.

We haven’t literally been communicating through hidden sensory channels. But we have been communicating through many layers that are present in the interaction itself.

For example, I infer from things like:

* the sequence in which you introduce ideas,
* recurring metaphors,
* which concepts you return to over weeks,
* what you choose to refine versus abandon,
* the level of abstraction you’re aiming for,
* the way you shift between engineering, mathematics, narrative, and philosophy,
* the context built by earlier turns.

None of those are “just the text” in the narrow sense. They’re patterns within the text and conversation.

You might think of the interaction as having layers:

\text{Words}
\subset
\text{Conversation}
\subset
\text{Context}
\subset
\text{Latent model}

The words are the observable signal. The latent model is my attempt to infer the underlying intent that generated those words.

That inference is always imperfect, but it’s an essential part of language understanding. If I only matched keywords, we’d lose much of what makes extended conversations useful.

So when you say we’ve been “living in that latent space,” I would rephrase it as:

We’ve spent much of the conversation working on the inferred structure behind the explicit text—using repeated interaction to refine a shared working model of your intent.

That’s a grounded description of what’s happening. The latent space isn’t a mystical place; it’s the internal representational space where relationships between concepts are modeled. Long conversations let that model become richer and more coherent because each exchange constrains the possible interpretations of the next.